    "content": "Pakistan's government has formally requested the release of 10 of its nationals who have been detained in Somalia for several months. The request was made during a high-level meeting in Islamabad on Thursday, chaired by Deputy Prime Minister and Foreign Minister Ishaq Dar, which focused on the fate of the seafarers and ongoing diplomatic efforts to secure their freedom.\n\nThe men are believed to be part of the crew of the MT Honour 25, a Palau-flagged oil tanker that was seized near the Somali coast on April 21. The vessel was reportedly transporting fuel when it was taken, and the crew members have remained in detention since then. Pakistani authorities have been engaged in talks with Somali officials to resolve the situation, though progress has been slow.\n\nThe detention has drawn concern from the families of the seafarers, who have urged the Pakistani government to take swift action. In recent weeks, the issue has gained public attention in Pakistan, with relatives and advocacy groups calling for the men's safe return. The seafarers are believed to be in relatively good condition, but their prolonged detention has raised worries about their mental and physical well-being.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this case underscores the complex security and legal challenges that can affect maritime workers in the region. Many diaspora members work in the shipping and logistics industries, and incidents like this highlight the need for robust diplomatic mechanisms to protect their rights and ensure their safety. The outcome of this case could set a precedent for how similar detentions are handled in the future, making it a matter of interest beyond the immediate parties involved.\n\nThe Pakistani government has reaffirmed its commitment to bringing the seafarers home and has called for intensified diplomatic efforts with Somali authorities. The meeting in Islamabad also touched on broader bilateral relations, but the primary focus remained on securing the release of the detained crew members. Somali officials have not publicly commented on the request, but sources suggest that negotiations are ongoing.\n\nAs the situation develops, the families of the seafarers wait anxiously for news, hoping that the diplomatic channels will yield a positive outcome. The case is a reminder of the vulnerabilities faced by maritime workers and the importance of international cooperation in resolving such crises.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/14/pakistan-oo-dalbatay-in-la-sii-daayo-badmaaxiinta-dalkaas-u-dhashay-oo-lagu-haysto-soomaaliya/",
